RabbitMQ separates publisher acceptance from consumer delivery:

- [[b:Publisher confirms]] tell a publisher whether the broker accepted a publish according to the chosen queue type and durability/replication semantics. Track sequence numbers asynchronously or in bounded batches for throughput; a confirm is not an end-to-end processing acknowledgement.
- [[b:Returns]] with the `mandatory` flag report a publish that could not be routed. A message can be returned and still receive a positive publisher confirm, so handle both callbacks.
- [[b:Consumer acknowledgements]] tell RabbitMQ whether a delivery may be removed or should be redelivered. A manual ack should follow the protected processing step, but an ack cannot prove that an external effect was correct.
- [[b:Prefetch]] bounds unacknowledged deliveries. Tune it from handler cost/fairness/memory; a value that improves throughput for uniform work can starve peers or amplify memory for large/skewed messages.

:::deep
Durability is a chain: durable topology, persistent-message intent, queue-type replication, publisher confirm policy, storage/node failure model and broker configuration.

For quorum queues, confirm semantics include the queue's replicated acceptance; no finite configuration guarantees survival of every correlated node/storage loss, operator deletion or expired message. Test the exact queue type and failure scenario.

After a database side effect, a crash before ack causes redelivery; therefore the consumer needs atomic inbox/deduplication with that state change. Ack-before-commit risks loss.

Publisher confirms also do not close database → broker dual write; use an Outbox (08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q7). The async confirm bookkeeping pattern is expanded in (25-microservice.04-messaging-event-driven-at-scale.q4).

**Runnable Java client class (requires the RabbitMQ Java client):**
<pre><code>import com.rabbitmq.client.*;
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.util.concurrent.*;
public class RabbitRoundTrip {
  static void handleIdempotently(byte[] body) {
    System.out.println(new String(body, StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
  }
  public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
    ConnectionFactory f = new ConnectionFactory();
    f.setHost("localhost");
    try (Connection c = f.newConnection(); Channel ch = c.createChannel()) {
      ch.queueDeclare("orders", true, false, false, null);
      ch.confirmSelect();
      ch.basicPublish("", "orders", MessageProperties.PERSISTENT_TEXT_PLAIN,
          "order-42".getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
      ch.waitForConfirmsOrDie(5_000);                 // publish confirmed
      ch.basicQos(20);                               // at most 20 unacked
      CountDownLatch done = new CountDownLatch(1);
      ch.basicConsume("orders", false, (tag, msg) -&gt; {
        try {
          handleIdempotently(msg.getBody());         // replace with dedup + commit
          ch.basicAck(msg.getEnvelope().getDeliveryTag(), false);
        } catch (Exception e) {
          ch.basicNack(msg.getEnvelope().getDeliveryTag(), false, false);
        } finally { done.countDown(); }
      }, tag -&gt; {});
      if (!done.await(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS)) throw new TimeoutException();
    }
  }
}</code></pre>

Start a local broker, then run this class from a project containing `com.rabbitmq:amqp-client`; it prints `order-42`.

A publisher confirm means the broker accepted responsibility; it does **not** mean a consumer completed the business effect. Ack only after the idempotent effect commits.

- **Simple path:** waiting per message is easy to reason about but limits throughput.
- **Fast path:** batching or asynchronous confirms increase throughput while adding in-flight correlation, memory and nack-recovery complexity.
:::
